Incident Report

Detection The incident was surfaced by correlating three separate log entries in /ccse/app's auth stream rather than by any single alert. A query of auth.log showed four LOGIN_FAIL events for user admin, all originating from IP 203.0.113.9, within a 12-second window (09:01:10–09:01:18).

Analysis The same IP that generated the four failed logins produced a LOGIN_OK four seconds after the last failure, followed shortly by an EXPORT_DATA event transferring 500MB. Running the correlation check (FAILS≥3 AND SUCCESS≥1 AND EXPORT≥1 for the same IP) returned fails=4, success=1, export=1, triggering the alert: "probable brute-force → compromise → data exfiltration." No individual log line was inherently malicious; the pattern only became visible when the three event types were correlated by source IP.

Containment The attacker IP 203.0.113.9 was blocked at the network layer using an iptables DROP rule on all inbound traffic from that address, preventing further access attempts or additional data exfiltration from the same source.

Evidence & Integrity Prior to containment, auth.log was chained into a SHA-256 hash chain (auth.chain), where each entry's hash depends on both its own content and the previous entry's hash. A tamper test (changing 500MB to 5MB in a copy of the log) produced a completely different final hash (72f1d537... vs. the original ababa787...), confirming the chain reliably detects alteration. A timestamped evidence copy (evidence_20260910.log) was also taken and hashed (sha256sum) to preserve chain-of-custody; the hash was independently re-verified (sha256sum -c) and returned OK.

Lesson Learned No single log line indicated compromise — brute-force attempts, a login, and a data export are each unremarkable in isolation. Effective detection required centralising logs and correlating multiple event types by a shared attribute (source IP) over a time window, reinforcing that a SIEM-style correlation approach catches incidents that per-line monitoring would miss.

Short-Answer Questions

Q1. What is the difference between a log and an event? Give an example of each from this lab. A log is a durable, stored record of something that happened, which can be queried at any later time — e.g., the line 2025-03-01T09:01:10 LOGIN_FAIL user=admin ip=203.0.113.9 stored in auth.log/CloudWatch Logs. An event is a real-time trigger fired the moment a condition is met — e.g., the correlation script's output ALERT: probable brute-force -> compromise -> data exfiltration, generated live when the fail/success/export thresholds were crossed, rather than being a static record.

Q2. Why must audit logs be tamper-proof, and how does a hash chain achieve this? Audit logs must be tamper-proof because they are often the only record of what an attacker did, and an attacker with system access will typically try to edit or delete logs to hide their tracks — an unprotected log cannot be trusted as evidence. A hash chain achieves this by making each entry's hash a function of its own content and the previous entry's hash. Changing any single entry changes its hash, which cascades and changes every hash after it in the chain, so the final hash no longer matches the original — making any alteration immediately detectable without needing to inspect every line manually.

Q3. How did correlation detect an incident that no single log line revealed? Individually, four failed logins could be a typo, one successful login is routine, and one data export is normal admin activity. None of these alone is conclusive evidence of an attack. Correlation combined all three signals — repeated failures, followed by a success, followed by an export, all from the same source IP within a short time window — into a single meaningful pattern (brute-force → compromise → exfiltration) that no individual entry could show on its own.

Q4. List the incident-response steps you performed and the goal of each.

Detect — correlate log events to identify the suspicious IP and confirm an incident occurred.
Contain — block the attacker's IP (iptables DROP) to stop further unauthorized access or exfiltration.
Collect evidence — make a timestamped, hashed copy of the log to preserve an untampered record for investigation.
Document — record the incident timeline and findings (this report) so the incident can be reviewed, reported, and learned from.

Q5. How do the same logs serve both security monitoring and compliance evidence? The same log data serves two purposes at once: for security monitoring, it enables real-time or near-real-time detection of malicious patterns (as in Task 5's correlation alert). For compliance evidence, the same tamper-evident, hashed records (Task 4 and Task 6) demonstrate to auditors or regulators that access to systems and data was tracked, that the organisation can reconstruct what happened during an incident, and that the evidence itself has not been altered — satisfying requirements such as audit trails and data-integrity controls referenced in Weeks 6, 10 and 11.
